C = [
("h1", "3. The Test of Loyalty"),
("p", "Claim: Moses gives Israel a test that no sign can pass. A prophet whose sign comes true is still refused if he says, “Let us go after other gods” (Deuteronomy 13:2). Jeremiah applies the same test to Hananiah. The test of loyalty is about where the word leads, and it stands apart from the test of whether the word comes to pass."),
("sub", "3.1 A sign that comes to pass"),
("p", "Deuteronomy 13 begins with a case that looks, at first, as though it should be easy. “If there arise among you a prophet, or a dreamer of dreams, and giveth thee a sign or a wonder, and the sign or the wonder come to pass, whereof he spake unto thee, saying, Let us go after other gods, which thou hast not known, and let us serve them” (Deuteronomy 13:1, 2). The prophet gives a sign. He speaks of it beforehand, and it comes to pass. By any test that stopped there he would be vindicated."),
("p", "The next verse refuses him. “Thou shalt not hearken unto the words of that prophet, or that dreamer of dreams: for the LORD your God proveth you, to know whether ye love the LORD your God with all your heart and with all your soul” (Deuteronomy 13:3). The sign came to pass, and the prophet is to be refused. Moses gives the reason in the same verse. The sign is not evidence of the LORD’s sending. It is the LORD’s proving of the people. What is being tested is not the prophet’s power to foretell but Israel’s love for the LORD."),
("p", "That fact fixes the volume’s second position. A fulfilled sign cannot validate a word that leads from the LORD. Chapter 4 will argue that an unfulfilled word is enough to show that the LORD did not speak it (Deuteronomy 18:22). The two chapters together are lopsided on purpose: failure can condemn, but success alone cannot approve. The test of loyalty is what remains when the sign has come true."),
("sub", "3.2 The fixed point"),
("p", "The loyalty test needs something to measure by, and Moses names it in the next verse. “Ye shall walk after the LORD your God, and fear him, and keep his commandments, and obey his voice, and ye shall serve him, and cleave unto him” (Deuteronomy 13:4). The measure is the LORD’s commandments, the ones already given through Moses. This is the same Moses of Numbers 12:7, 8, to whom the LORD spoke mouth to mouth. A prophet who speaks against the commandments is refused whether or not his sign comes to pass, because the commandments came by plainer speech than any later dream."),
("p", "It follows that the prophets did not add commandments to the Law as though they were its new lawgivers. They called Israel back to the commandments, and they announced what the LORD would do in judgment and mercy. This is the pattern the volume has seen in Jeremiah 23:22: the true word “turned them from their evil way.” The prophets of the other kind turned them away from the LORD, and the test of loyalty detects that."),
("sub", "3.3 The sentence"),
("p", "The penalty is severe, and it is severe because of what the prophet has done. “And that prophet, or that dreamer of dreams, shall be put to death; because he hath spoken to turn you away from the LORD your God, which brought you out of the land of Egypt, and redeemed you out of the house of bondage, to thrust thee out of the way which the LORD thy God commanded thee to walk in” (Deuteronomy 13:5). The ground of the sentence is stated: he has spoken to turn Israel away from the LORD who redeemed them. The sentence is under the Law of Moses, to Israel in the land. The volume reports it as the Law’s sentence on that offence in that setting and carries it no further."),
("sub", "3.4 Hananiah"),
("p", "Jeremiah 28 shows the test at work. Hananiah speaks in the house of the LORD before the priests and the people. “Thus speaketh the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel, saying, I have broken the yoke of the king of Babylon” (Jeremiah 28:2). He names a term: “Within two full years will I bring again into this place all the vessels of the LORD’s house” (Jeremiah 28:3). He uses the LORD’s name, he speaks in the LORD’s house, and he speaks of the LORD’s own vessels. Nothing in his manner is crude. The people had every reason to believe him, because he said what they wished."),
("p", "Jeremiah’s answer, after the word of the LORD comes to him, is the test of loyalty in a sentence. “Hear now, Hananiah; The LORD hath not sent thee; but thou makest this people to trust in a lie” (Jeremiah 28:15). Then the ground: “because thou hast taught rebellion against the LORD” (Jeremiah 28:16). The Hebrew noun behind “rebellion” here is the one behind “turn you away” in Deuteronomy 13:5, and the sentence on Hananiah is the sentence of that chapter. The volume does not suppose that Jeremiah had the Deuteronomy passage open before him. It observes that he uses its charge and that Hananiah’s end follows its pattern: he died “the same year in the seventh month” (Jeremiah 28:17)."),
("p", "The words are the same as Deuteronomy 13 in what they judge. The charge is not that Hananiah mistook a date. The charge is that he made the people trust in a lie and taught rebellion against the LORD. The test applied is the test of source, “The LORD hath not sent thee,” joined to the test of loyalty, “rebellion against the LORD.” The test of fulfilment does not appear in the verdict at all. Hananiah’s death in the seventh month fell within the two months after the prophecy, and it is the LORD’s act, as the verdict says, not a measure of whether Hananiah’s two years had run."),
("sub", "3.5 Baal, and the forgetting of the name"),
("p", "The Jeremiah 23 charges already included this test. Samaria’s prophets “prophesied in Baal” (Jeremiah 23:13). The dreamers of Jerusalem thought “to cause my people to forget my name by their dreams which they tell every man to his neighbour, as their fathers have forgotten my name for Baal” (Jeremiah 23:27). Forgetting the LORD’s name for another is the charge of Deuteronomy 13, in the words of Jeremiah. The test of loyalty therefore stands under the test of source in Jeremiah’s own chapter. The prophet whose vision comes from his own heart ends by leading the people from the LORD, and the two tests catch the same offender from two sides."),
("ctbl", "The test of loyalty in Deuteronomy 13 and Jeremiah 28.", [
    ("Question", "Deuteronomy 13:1–5", "Jeremiah 28:15–17"),
    ("Is there a sign?", "Yes: the sign or the wonder comes to pass (13:2)", "A prophecy and a symbol: the yoke broken (28:2, 10)"),
    ("Where does the word lead?", "“Let us go after other gods” (13:2)", "The people “trust in a lie” (28:15)"),
    ("What is the people’s test?", "The LORD proves whether they love him (13:3)", "The word of the LORD to Jeremiah after the yoke is broken (28:12)"),
    ("What is the charge?", "“hath spoken to turn you away from the LORD” (13:5)", "“hast taught rebellion against the LORD” (28:16)"),
    ("What follows?", "The prophet is put to death (13:5)", "Hananiah dies the same year (28:17)"),
  ], [0.22, 0.39, 0.39]),
("sub", "3.6 To the law and to the testimony"),
("p", "Isaiah gives the test of loyalty its shortest form in a day when many voices claimed to speak for the dead and the unseen. “And when they shall say unto you, Seek unto them that have familiar spirits, and unto wizards that peep, and that mutter: should not a people seek unto their God? for the living to the dead?” (Isaiah 8:19). Then the rule: “To the law and to the testimony: if they speak not according to this word, it is because there is no light in them” (Isaiah 8:20)."),
("p", "The volume reads Isaiah 8:20 as the same rule as Deuteronomy 13, said by a prophet to his own generation. The measure is the law and the testimony, a fixed word already given. The verdict on a voice that fails it is not that it is mistaken or weak. It is that “there is no light in them.” The ground is the word given through Moses, and a later voice is held to it. The prophets do not ask Israel to believe them because they say so; they send Israel back to what the LORD has already said and ask whether the new word agrees."),
("p", "This is why the loyalty test works when a sign cannot. A sign can be borrowed or imitated, and Deuteronomy 13 allows that a sign may even come to pass. A fixed word cannot be imitated by a stranger without contradicting itself. The test is therefore the surest the prophets give for a day when feeling runs high and the crowd wants a word. It was the test Israel could apply without waiting for an outcome."),
("sub", "3.7 The prophets in Babylon"),
("p", "Jeremiah 29 shows the test applied to a second audience. The letter is written to the exiles, who had their own prophets and diviners, and the instruction is direct. “Let not your prophets and your diviners, that be in the midst of you, deceive you, neither hearken to your dreams which ye cause to be dreamed. For they prophesy falsely unto you in my name: I have not sent them, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 29:8, 9). Two tests stand together in one verse. The prophets speak “in my name,” which is the claim of sending, and the LORD answers “I have not sent them,” which is the test of source."),
("p", "Then the LORD names two men, Ahab the son of Kolaiah and Zedekiah the son of Maaseiah, “which prophesy a lie unto you in my name” (Jeremiah 29:21). The charge adds a second element to the lie. “They have committed villany in Israel, and have committed adultery with their neighbours’ wives, and have spoken lying words in my name, which I have not commanded them; even I know, and am a witness, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 29:23). Here the text itself joins conduct to the false word. The volume holds the order it has argued since chapter 2: conduct is evidence, the LORD is the witness, and the charge that decides is that the words were “not commanded.”"),
("p", "The captives themselves took up a curse from the case: “The LORD make thee like Zedekiah and like Ahab, whom the king of Babylon roasted in the fire” (Jeremiah 29:22). A false word given in the LORD’s name was remembered by a nation and made into a proverb. It shows how seriously Israel took the test of loyalty and source, and how long the memory of a failed prophet could last."),
("sub", "3.8 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "A sign that comes to pass does not validate a word that leads Israel from the LORD.",
    "The measure is the LORD’s commandments, given through Moses and fixed before any later prophet spoke.",
    "Failure can condemn a prophet and success alone cannot approve him; the test of loyalty remains when the sign has come true.",
    "Jeremiah answers Hananiah by the tests of source and loyalty; he does not wait for the two years to run.",
    "The prophets call Israel back to the commandments; they are not new lawgivers.",
    "Isaiah 8:20 sends every voice back “to the law and to the testimony”; Jeremiah 29 applies the same rule to the exiles’ prophets, and the LORD’s own witness settles the case.",
  ]),
]
